from write_record import record,progress,ROOT
record(25,'re',1,'Reading the condition on a subordinate invocation',
'Change my execution plan from treating every manifest invocation as mandatory to following the exact source’s branch conditions.',
'The allocation lists categorize→gg, and my initial handoff described gg as a required dependency. That was a plausible manifest-based interpretation; the original body is the authority for which branch invokes it.',
'''
Reading purpose: application, not general mastery. Questions: Does every categorize run invoke gg? What input branch is assigned here? What does 8x require? What stops a forced categorization? Deadline: before executing the two categorize applications.

Preview of the original categorize: interpretations precede gathering, six pattern sources, a three-test quality filter, assignment/naming, distribution output, patterns, summary, and depth scaling. The figure-like object is the cross-reference matrix; the depth table requires full content scan at 8x. The closing command does not override branch conditions.

Active reading answers: interpretation 3, abstract concept generation, invokes gg. Interpretation 1 inventories existing files; interpretation 2 categorizes existing output. The current applications have actual prior records and working views, so neither needs item generation. All six dimension sources must be checked. A candidate dimension must split into at least two groups with more than three items each, be orthogonal, and be non-obvious. A no-dimension result is legitimate. This changes a concrete allocation decision: gg is not executed solely because the manifest lists an edge.

Recall attempt without further tool retrieval: “Categorize existing items, inspect six sources of variation, require two groups with at least three items, and reject obvious or redundant dimensions.” Comparison with the loaded source finds one exact error: the threshold is more than three, so each qualifying group needs at least four, not three. The note is repaired. This is a current-context model recall check; the source remained in conversational context, so it is not a blind memory experiment.

Structured note: `Existing items → no generation dependency. Concept with no items → gg. At 8x read full content. Candidate dimension needs two groups of ≥4, distinct information, non-obvious result. No passing dimension → honest no-category outcome.` Source and requirements remain linked through the receipt.

Immediate review then applies the note to seven existing working views. Seven items cannot be split into two groups of at least four; no dimension can pass that numerical gate regardless of how appealing the labels are. That becomes the second categorization application’s input constraint. A scheduled within-run review is placed immediately before those applications; no calendar reminder or delayed human retention claim is made.

Certificate: exact claim—the manifest edge makes gg mandatory for these existing-output categorize runs—is rejected by the original’s explicit interpretation-3 condition. The strongest contrary is an input that actually lacks items and requests concept generation; that would require gg and its own dependencies. The repaired ≥4 threshold has an arithmetic consequence on the seven-item case. The reading procedure changes two concrete execution choices, not merely my description of the source.
''',
'I removed the unnecessary gg invocation for the two existing-item applications and corrected a recalled threshold from ≥3 to ≥4 before applying it.',
'The actual execution plan now follows the original branch and avoids generating substitute items for an existing corpus. The threshold correction prevents a false categorization pass.',
'KEEP — source-conditioned execution and corrected numerical reading.',
'The compact conditional note is the working entry for categorize; the full original remains the authority and is reread if the input branch changes.',
'Apply the note to the full corpus and seven-view subset; test a genuine concept-generation request separately; inspect another manifest edge for an input-dependent condition.',
'No numerical 8x floor exists. All seven reading operations were performed with preview, four questions, section answers, an explicit recall error and repair, structured note, immediate application, and a within-run review commitment; no delayed-memory or human learning effect is claimed.')
record(26,'re',2,'Reading an example as evidence of what, exactly?',
'Test whether active reading of the scope and state cards changes the model’s ability to apply their limits on a different specimen.',
'The distinctions in 012 and 013 were already available and used. The strongest ordinary continuation is direct lookup. I wanted to test whether a recall-and-review pass adds anything beyond that availability.',
'''
Purpose: application and transfer within the present model context. Questions: What does a mixed vector refute? What does an unknown cell leave open? When does an empty domain alter an entailment? What does changed wording fail to establish? The material is a short pair of local records, with no deadline requiring a slower human study schedule.

Preview: 012 begins from near-identical sentences and uses a mixed case, all-zero/all-one cases, and empty-domain boundary. 013 begins from two observed positives and separates missing evidence from false outcome. Both end with narrower conclusions than the most tempting paraphrase. Existing knowledge includes the broad instruction to preserve scope; the expected new task is applying that instruction with exact countermodels.

Questions answered during reading: “not every” is refuted only by all true in the declared domain; “every not” is false if any true; an unknown middle value admits both completions; neither lack of data nor fluent paraphrase establishes cause. No psychological claims from the RE overview are treated as findings about this model or a human.

Recall without another tool read: On `[0,?]`, “every improved” is false because the observed zero is decisive; “none improved” remains unresolved because the unknown may be one. Check against the original distinction confirms the answer. A second case `[1,?]` makes “some improved” true and “every improved” unresolved. The two cases are direct substitutions into already-used rules, not independent delayed transfer.

Structured note: one observed false defeats universal success; one observed true establishes existential success; unknown leaves only propositions that depend on it unresolved. The empty-domain caveat is retained separately and not forced into every ordinary nonempty example. Immediate review on `[?]` leaves every/some/none unresolved under a fixed one-member domain. A within-run later lookup is assigned to the state-taxonomy validation. No next-day, week, or month review has occurred.

Strong alternative: direct lookup of 013 yields the same three outputs with less generated note material. The active-reading sequence found no new error and no capability absent from the baseline. Certificate: claim—this second RE pass adds a useful local distinction or operation beyond the existing lookup—is unsupported by the equal outcomes. The strongest favorable branch is future recall without lookup; that evidence is absent. The correct result is unchanged working knowledge, with delayed benefit unresolved rather than self-rated as learned.
''',
'I correctly applied the existing rules to three new vectors but found no new distinction, repair, or demonstrated advantage over direct lookup.',
'Current-context application succeeds; incremental retention or access benefit is unestablished. The source remaining in context prevents an independent memory claim.',
'UNRESOLVED — incremental reading/retention benefit.',
'The concise decisive-observation note is retained as a candidate cue, with the original scope cards still sufficient for lookup.',
'Check a later use after genuinely different intervening work; compare lookup cost only if it matters; test a compound proposition where two unknowns interact.',
'No numerical 8x floor exists. All seven reading operations are represented, with explicit purpose/preview/questions, active answers, recall-and-check, three distinct cases, structured notes and within-run later-use plan. Delayed review effects remain unperformed.')
record(27,'cn',1,'Case-first does not win the early-exit comparison',
'Compare case-before-rule and rule-before-case encounters on the exact file-existence question retained in application 010.',
'The case-first route was a promising candidate for exposing an unnoticed assumption. Rule-first is a strong alternative when the reader wants a usable answer immediately. Neither had been compared on this concrete encounter.',
'''
Goal: inform a reader inspecting whether a local file establishes future retrieval. Audience: the current model reviewer, with potential human use untested. Three instantiated orderings follow.

Pyramid: “A local file’s existence does not establish future retrieval. It can be opened now, while no later encounter has yet occurred. Record actual later use separately from existence and saving.”

Narrative arc: “A file is present and can be opened now. The next task arrives later. If no one opens the file then, the earlier successful save has still occurred. The missing fact is later retrieval.”

Layered: “Present access and later retrieval are different events. Now: the file exists and opens. Later: the relevant reader must actually find and use it. The source record preserves the separate states.”

Flow checks: pyramid states conclusion→specific reason→record consequence; arc gives present event→later event→countercase→missing fact; layered gives distinction→current detail→later detail→provenance. Removing formatting preserves those distinct argumentative functions. No decorative section break supplies the relationship.

First-20% check, counted by words: the pyramid’s first portion includes the negative retrieval conclusion; the arc’s first portion reports current file access without the missing later event; layered begins with the two-event distinction. All final full texts preserve the correct answer. The source’s claim that final information is what readers remember is not tested here and is not reported as an effect.

Actual local disposition: for the immediate question, select pyramid. For an exploratory dialogue asking the reader to inspect their own inference, retain the arc as a candidate. The source-audit order from 003 remains separate; this is an encounter comparison. Certificate: claim—case-first is superior for this current answer-seeking encounter—is rejected by the early-exit information loss and the user’s answer-first need. Strong contrary—case-first may create a useful question for an initially uninterested reader; no such reader is observed. The finding mostly applies the existing answer-first and scoped-entry standards, so it earns no additional independent KEEP.
''',
'I ran the concrete sequence comparison proposed in 010 and chose the conclusion-first encounter for the current inspection task.',
'The current encounter exposes the needed answer earlier; added engagement or human memory benefit is untested, and the choice repeats an established access rule.',
'REJECT — proposed general advantage of case-first for this task.',
'Three complete short encounters stay side by side; the pyramid is the current entry, with the arc reserved as an untested exploratory alternative.',
'Test an actual reader who has not yet formed the question; compare early exit after a full sentence rather than percentage; test a case where a premature conclusion would hide a live trade-off.',
'No numerical 8x floor exists. Goal/audience were fixed, three materially different complete orders were applied, flow and formatting independence checked, full and first-20% content compared, and the selected order used for the actual question.')
record(28,'cn',2,'A comparison order that keeps a live trade-off in its opening',
'Create an encounter that lets the reader see why two representations remain viable under different criteria before choosing one.',
'Application 023 gives A=two cases and B=three required cases. A conclusion-first “B wins” would conceal A’s compactness advantage unless the criterion is in the opening; a chronological account would delay the live choice.',
'''
Goal: compare. Audience: a model reviewer choosing the next working representation, already familiar with the cases. Candidate compare-and-contrast text:

“B covers all three required cases; A uses two. If full coverage is required, use B. If this task only needs the two shared cases, A is sufficient and shorter. The observed counts stay the same when the criterion changes.”

Candidate pyramid text:

“Use B for full three-case coverage. It includes the case that A omits. A remains the shorter option when the omitted case is outside the current task. Neither choice follows from length alone.”

Candidate layered text:

“The choice depends on whether the third case is required. B contains three cases and A contains two. For full coverage B is sufficient and A is not; for the two-case task both are sufficient and A uses fewer cases.”

Each has a different first act: paired properties, conditional recommendation, or decision crux. The current task has a live uncertainty about whether the third case is required, so the layered opening is selected. First-20% inspection finds the crux in its opening, whereas the pyramid begins a conditional recommendation and the comparison begins the facts. All full versions preserve the smaller option’s legitimate case. The strongest ordinary alternative is the exact four-field card from consolidation 04; it already carries the trade-off and is sufficient for audit.

Actual use: the next design task is a one-line excerpt, which does not require all three explanatory cases. Under the selected opening question, the third case is not required for that excerpt; A is permitted as a display while the full B remains the evidence source. This is a concrete change of presentation scope, not a change to the observed outcome or a claim that shorter text always wins.

Certificate: claim—the crux-first opening can preserve a live choice without burying the current uncertainty—is supported by the complete opening and the actual excerpt decision. Strong contrary—when full coverage is explicitly required, the pyramid is more direct; preserved as the alternate. The evidence supports a local task-conditioned sequence, not a general reader-memory effect. Because this operationalizes the already-kept criterion distinction rather than adding a new substantive finding, it is recorded as local uptake without fresh keep credit.
''',
'I selected a crux-first opening and used it to permit a two-case excerpt while retaining the full three-case source.',
'Local uptake of the existing criterion-dependent comparison is demonstrated; incremental benefit beyond that keep is not separately established.',
'REJECT — additional KEEP credit for a repeated criterion-sensitive ordering; usable local uptake retained.',
'The three encounter variants are retained by reader task: explicit full-coverage ask→pyramid; unresolved third-case need→crux-first; audit→four-field card.',
'Test a task where the third case changes the conclusion; test a reader asking only for the recommendation; compare crux-first with a single question only when a user answer would change the action.',
'No numerical 8x floor exists. Goal/audience, three complete orderings, flow, first-20% preservation, countertask and a later excerpt choice were executed; no universal sequencing effect is claimed.')
